Day 63 - Reverse Nodes in k-Group

Problem Statement:
Given the head of a linked list, reverse the nodes k at a time and return the modified list.

If the number of remaining nodes is less than k, those nodes should remain unchanged.

Only the links between nodes can be modified; node values must not be changed.

Code

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */


class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* reverseKGroup(ListNode* head, int k) {
        ListNode* temp = head;


        // Check whether k nodes are available
        int count = 0;


        while (count < k) {
            if (temp == NULL) {
                return head;
            }


            temp = temp->next;
            count++;
        }


        // Reverse the remaining groups first
        ListNode* prevNode = reverseKGroup(temp, k);


        // Reverse the current k nodes
        count = 0;
        temp = head;
        ListNode* next = NULL;


        while (count < k) {
            next = temp->next;


            temp->next = prevNode;
            prevNode = temp;
            temp = next;


            count++;
        }


        return prevNode;
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(n)

Space Complexity:
O(n/k) due to the recursion stack.

Approach:
The solution uses recursion + linked-list reversal.

For every group:

First check whether at least k nodes are available.
If fewer than k nodes remain, return head without modifying them.

Recursively process the remaining list first:

prevNode = reverseKGroup(temp, k);
Reverse the current k nodes using the standard three-pointer technique:
temp → current node
prevNode → previously processed node
next → saves the next node
During reversal, the last node of the current group is connected to the already-processed remaining groups.
Return the new head of the reversed group.

